"Combines a quartile box and median with whiskers extending to the full observed minimum and maximum.\n\nUse when: Full observed range matters and must remain visible for every group. Readers can be told explicitly that whiskers use min/max rather than Tukey fences.\n\nAvoid when: Extreme observations would compress the quartile boxes beyond usefulness.\n\nVisual grammar: The box encodes the middle 50% and its median. Whisker endpoints encode the smallest and largest observed values.\n\nBuild: Calculate each group’s minimum, quartiles, median, and maximum. Draw equal-width boxes and extend whiskers to the observed extremes. Label the min/max convention prominently and include group sample sizes.\n\nCheck: Check endpoint values equal the actual included extremes. Verify quartiles and medians fall inside their correct ordered intervals. Distinguish missing observations from observed values in the summary.\n\nPitfalls: Readers familiar with Tukey plots may incorrectly infer an outlier rule. Full range depends strongly on sample size and does not represent population bounds.\n\nExample: Compare synthetic sensor operating ranges while retaining median and quartiles for each device batch.\n\nAlternatives: Box plot, Range bar chart, Letter-value plot."
